  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-RIEMANN-INFORMATION-INTERFACE-INTERPRETED-PRODUCTION-20260908",
  "source_sha256": "b3adec0d3616dbde57a5522cfce1861890887d7c03a2232d2136cb94c9bac1d5",
  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, §1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation law is d/dt ∫[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)). Each component of q measures the density of a conserved quantity; its total amount between the endpoints changes only through endpoint flux."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, §1.1.2; raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, continuation",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth; integral conservation law (1.10) is the more fundamental equation",
      "observation": "Sufficient smoothness permits deriving differential equation (1.8) from (1.10). Solutions can contain discontinuities, including shock waves, and nonlinear conservation laws can develop discontinuities from smooth initial data. At a discontinuity the differential equation does not hold classically, while the integral conservation law continues to hold."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, §1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "cell average of q, which is this integral divided by the volume of the cell",
      "observation": "Finite volume methods use the integral conservation law, partition the domain into grid cells, and approximate cell integrals or normalized cell averages. Each time step modifies these values through fluxes at cell edges."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, §1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "numerical flux functions ... based on the approximate cell averages, the only data available",
      "observation": "The principal computational problem is obtaining numerical flux functions that approximate the correct fluxes reasonably well from approximate cell averages."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, §1.2.1, equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "the hyperbolic equation together with special initial data",
      "observation": "A Riemann problem consists of the hyperbolic equation with piecewise constant initial data having a single jump, placed at x = 0: q(x,0) = q_l for x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for x > 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, §1.2.1, paragraph immediately following (1.11)",
      "anchor": "q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i",
      "observation": "For cell averages Q_{i−1} and Q_i in neighboring finite volume cells, solving the Riemann problem with these left and right states provides information usable to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, §1.2.1, final paragraph; raw PDF page 28, printed page 6, continuation",
      "anchor": "typically a similarity solution, a function of x/t alone",
      "observation": "The text describes the typical hyperbolic Riemann solution as a similarity solution comprising finitely many waves propagating from the origin at constant wave speeds. It describes solving linear systems using eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A, refers to Chapter 3, and discusses nonlinear solutions with references to Chapters 11 and 13."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 28, printed page 6, §1.2.1, final paragraph",
      "anchor": "approximate Riemann solvers are used",
      "observation": "Exact nonlinear Riemann solutions are often too expensive computationally; numerical methods therefore use approximate Riemann solvers, developed in §15.3."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "q(x,t) denotes the state, whose components are conserved-quantity densities, and f denotes its flux function; x and t are spatial and temporal arguments. [Printed p. 4, §1.1.1, (1.10) and its explanation.]",
      "x₁ and x₂ are arbitrary interval endpoints in the stated integral conservation law. The displayed equation does not separately state an ordering condition. [Printed p. 4, §1.1.1, 'for any two points'.]",
      "Grid cells partition the domain; Q_{i−1} and Q_i denote cell averages in two neighboring cells. The index range, grid extent, and boundary treatment are not specified here. [Printed p. 5, §§1.2–1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]",
      "q_l and q_r are constant left and right Riemann states; for the neighboring-cell construction they are assigned Q_{i−1} and Q_i, respectively. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, (1.11) and following paragraph.]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The setting is a conservation law with state q and flux f satisfying the integral balance (1.10); finite volume methods are based on this integral formulation. [Printed pp. 4–5, §§1.1.1–1.2.]",
      "Derivation of the differential equation from the integral equation requires sufficient smoothness of q and f(q). This condition belongs to that derivation, not to the subsequent description of discontinuous solutions. [Printed p. 4, §1.1.2, opening paragraph.]",
      "The Riemann construction uses the hyperbolic equation and initial data constant on each side of a single jump. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, opening paragraph and (1.11).]",
      "For the stated neighboring-cell workflow, Q_{i−1} and Q_i are cell averages in adjacent finite volume cells and become the left and right Riemann data. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "Integral conservation remains the governing balance for discontinuous solutions even where the differential equation fails in the classical sense. [Printed p. 5, §1.1.2, continuation.]",
      "Finite volume methods approximate cell integrals or their normalization by cell volume and modify these quantities each time step using fluxes through cell edges. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Numerical fluxes are to approximate the correct fluxes using the available approximate cell averages. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Solving the Riemann problem with q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i yields information that can be used to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step. This is a description of a computational construction, without a displayed update formula or quantitative accuracy theorem. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "The integral balance has inflow-minus-outflow sign convention: f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)). [Printed p. 4, (1.10) and total-mass explanation.]",
      "A cell average is the integral of q over a cell divided by that cell's volume. Treating normalization as essential is explicit in the source. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "The jump location is chosen as x = 0 for the Riemann problem. Initial values are specified on x < 0 and x > 0; (1.11) supplies no value at x = 0. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, (1.11).]",
      "A Riemann problem includes the hyperbolic equation as well as its special initial data. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, opening sentence.]",
      "A similarity solution is described as a function of x/t alone. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The discussion accommodates a vector q with componentwise conserved quantities and nonlinear flux functions. [Printed p. 4, opening continuation and §1.1.1.]",
      "Processes that do not conserve the quantity, such as chemical reactions, require source terms. The displayed endpoint-flux balance concerns the conservation setting without those additional terms. [Printed p. 4, §1.1.1, contaminant example.]",
      "The motivation for the integral formulation is that solutions can develop shocks and other discontinuities, including from smooth initial data for nonlinear conservation laws. [Printed pp. 4–5, §1.1.2.]",
      "The description permits approximate Riemann solvers in numerical implementations, particularly because exact nonlinear solves may be expensive. [Printed p. 6, §1.2.1, final paragraph.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Physical and numerical flux roles",
        "details": [
          "f in (1.10) is the physical flux function evaluated at endpoint states. [Printed p. 4, §1.1.1.]",
          "A numerical flux approximates the correct flux based on approximate cell averages; the source does not identify it with f without an intervening numerical construction. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Information obtained from a Riemann solve",
        "details": [
          "The text states the dependency from neighboring averages through a Riemann solve to information usable for numerical flux computation and a time-step update. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]",
          "It does not specify the mathematical type of this information, an interface sampling rule, or whether a complete solution must be retained. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, same paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Scope of descriptive solution properties",
        "details": [
          "The similarity and wave description is qualified by 'typically'; the passage does not state it as an unqualified theorem for every hyperbolic problem. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, final paragraph.]",
          "Linear Riemann solutions are described through eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A, with development deferred to Chapter 3; nonlinear theory is deferred to Chapters 11 and 13. [Printed pp. 5–6, §1.2.1.]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the distinction between classical differential validity and the integral conservation law that continues to hold for discontinuous solutions. [Printed pp. 4–5, §1.1.2.]",
    "Preserve cell-volume normalization in the definition of cell average. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]",
    "Preserve the neighboring-cell assignments q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i, including their order. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]",
    "A Riemann problem must contain the hyperbolic equation and the specified piecewise constant initial data. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, opening paragraph.]",
    "Do not assign a source-mandated value to q(0,0), because neither branch of (1.11) covers it. [Printed p. 5, (1.11).]",
    "Preserve the stated use of Riemann-solution information for numerical flux computation and updating cell averages; the passage does not supply a unique flux or update algorithm. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]",
    "Do not turn the qualitative phrase 'approximate ... reasonably well' into a source-stated error bound, convergence rate, or stability result. [Printed p. 5, §1.2, first paragraph.]",
    "Do not turn the qualified typical similarity-solution description into a universal assertion. [Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, final paragraph.]"
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The mathematical meaning of the information supplied by a Riemann solve is unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, §1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11), states only that information can be obtained and used to compute a numerical flux and update averages.",
      "impact": "The source does not select a data structure, interface trace, wave decomposition, or particular map from a solution to a flux."
    },
    {
      "issue": "No particular numerical flux or time-step update is defined.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, §1.2 describes edge-flux updates qualitatively; §1.2.1 describes the Riemann-based workflow without displaying either formula.",
      "impact": "A particular flux formula, spatial or temporal normalization in the update, time-step restriction, or stencil beyond the stated neighboring input pair would require additional source evidence."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The initial state at the jump is omitted.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, (1.11), has branches only for x < 0 and x > 0.",
      "impact": "The source does not choose a left, right, or other value at x = 0."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Regularity and the precise interpretation of integral conservation at discontinuities are not formalized here.",
      "evidence": "Printed pp. 4–5, §1.1.2 uses 'sufficiently smooth' for differential derivation and says (1.10) continues to hold at discontinuities, without specifying trace or time-derivative conventions.",
      "impact": "The passage does not determine function spaces, endpoint trace choices, or whether temporal identities are classical, almost everywhere, or interpreted weakly."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Existence, uniqueness, and admissibility conditions for Riemann solutions are not stated in the selected passage.",
      "evidence": "Printed pp. 5–6, §1.2.1 describes solving the problem and refers to later chapters for theory.",
      "impact": "The workflow should not be strengthened into unconditional unique solvability for arbitrary equations and states."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The status of the neighboring averages as exact or approximate is not repeated in the workflow sentence.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, §1.2 calls the available data approximate cell averages; §1.2.1 subsequently calls Q_{i−1} and Q_i cell averages.",
      "impact": "The mathematical normalization is explicit, but identifying every numerical Q_i with the exact average of an exact solution is not justified by this passage."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "The integral conservation law balances the change of the conserved state integrated over an interval against its endpoint fluxes (printed p. 4, (1.10)). This remains fundamental for discontinuous solutions, where the differential equation fails classically (printed pp. 4–5, §1.1.2). Finite volume methods approximate integrals over grid cells, or cell averages obtained by dividing those integrals by cell volumes, and update them through numerical edge fluxes based on the available approximate averages (printed p. 5, §1.2). Given averages Q_{i−1} and Q_i in neighboring cells, use them as the left and right constant states of a Riemann problem for the hyperbolic equation. Solving that problem supplies information usable to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step (printed p. 5, §1.2.1, (1.11) and following paragraph). The passage leaves the information representation, flux formula, update formula, and value of the initial state at the jump unspecified. It also recognizes approximate Riemann solvers in numerical implementations (printed p. 6, §1.2.1)."
}
